WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.929 --> 00:00:04.096
- [Narrator] This is a lined seahorse.

00:00:05.103 --> 00:00:06.673
A naturally poor swimmer,

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.793
the lined seahorse is often found latched

00:00:08.793 --> 00:00:13.127
to aquatic vegetation like
seagrass or sargassum.

00:00:13.127 --> 00:00:14.448
Rather than hunt,

00:00:14.448 --> 00:00:18.554
it camouflages itself and
waits for it's prey to pass.

00:00:18.554 --> 00:00:21.287
It's eyes can move
independently of one another

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:24.699
allowing it to scan it's surroundings.

00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:26.794
It's habitat is extensive.

00:00:26.794 --> 00:00:29.433
It can be found along the
Eastern coast of the Americas,

00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:31.177
as north as Nova Scotia,

00:00:31.177 --> 00:00:33.117
and as south as Uraguay.

00:00:33.117 --> 00:00:34.656
They're quite small reaching only

00:00:34.656 --> 00:00:37.279
up to six inches in length.

00:00:37.279 --> 00:00:39.635
Because of coastal growth and pollution

00:00:39.635 --> 00:00:41.609
the natural habitat of the lined seahorse

00:00:41.609 --> 00:00:43.359
has been diminishing.

00:00:44.636 --> 00:00:47.779
This, paired with it's common
use in Chinese medicine

00:00:47.779 --> 00:00:49.825
and the aquarium trade,
has seen it's numbers

00:00:49.825 --> 00:00:52.492
dwindling since the late 1990's.

00:00:53.485 --> 00:00:55.735
This is the lined seahorse.

